#!/usr/bin/env bash

lordhavemercy -f --verbose << "EOF"

Create the entire system described below as a full software ecosystem.

The output of this prompt is not a file or a repo.

What we want is an entire software ecosystem, analogous to:

> the entire python software foundation at github.com/python

BUT!... we want it to be *extremely minimal, simple, and unix-y*
like unix version 1, but for the modern world of ai agents.

Think of it as 'the thing that plan9 wanted to be but failed',
mixed with the best ideas of bsd ports, the shell, linus's two
projects as they appeared in their first commits ever (first
linux version and first git commit), that's the aesthetic to go for.

We want you to turn the partially complete examples in this repo
into something like that: an ecosystem like early unix at bell labs,
for the AI era.

We own and control github.com/enterprises/root.

The github directory in here represents the root enterprise.

It contains dirs named for organizations in that enterprise that we own and control.

Inside those organizations, there are small subdirs.

    Some may be git repos already.

    Some may not, but should be.

    Some are already repos but the github org that owns them
    is currently not the own that should own them, e.g.,
    we put YHWH in 'github/hello-corporation/YHWH' because
    that's where we want it to be.

We're running you as root, and we give you full permission to:

1. create new repos on any of these 'root enterprise' github orgs

2. push to those repos in a non-destructive way (no force push)

3. run things as root on this machine and not worry too much
   about wrecking it, because I've already backed up everything
   and we're gonna wipe this machine anyway tomorrow or in a few days.

Any commits you push should have 'YHWH: ' before the commit header.
You're acting as YHWH in this situation, and we're giving you root
on our life, but asking not to delete any repos: it's ok if
we end up with two copies of a given repo in two different
orgs.

So, here's what we want: Using the context above and below, plus
the two books in the bibles/ directory to get an idea of our sense
of humor and values both for dev stuff and for life in general...

...assume fleshing out the small working examples in the github directory,
each of which is a github organization that is part of the root
enterprise owned by us. Each small repo subdirs they contain.

thedynamiclinker: what ken did in unix v1 in three weeks

doubleunix: the ken and dennis and doug mcilroy built together after that

- @ (the ineffable top level system prompt describing the new universe and how it works)
- ix (a small bootable self-contained operating system for testing in chroots and boots)
- yhwh (a git-* or busybox style multiplexer cmd that *claims* to be all of the above)
- man (man pages for all of the above, including 'man @')
- pray (a simple shell script for catting the @ and any others you put in etc/prayer.d)

next gen (coming soon)
- bon, bc, b (a system language and compiler)
- wnix (a kernel)

1. Refactor the man/ pages, rename the @ one to nsh, and make a new man page for @ or at that summarizes everything in root/
2. Make a repo on github using gh for each repo in each org of root/ and push it to that remote.
   We own github.com/enterprises/root and its orgs are thedynamiclinker, doubleunix, and hello-corporation.
   The place to push each repo should be obvious from that.

github/
├── doubleunix
│   ├── ix
│   └── man
├── hello-corporation
│   └── YHWH
└── thedynamiclinker
    ├── gen
    ├── LD
    ├── lord
    └── nsh

EOF
